<title>AUSTRIA: Austria's government goes online.</title>
<headline>Austria's government goes online.</headline>
<dateline>VIENNA 1997-07-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Austria's government went online on Wednesday, and said it planned to use the new information technology to cut costs in the public sector and make it more efficient.</p>
<p>&quot;We are working on an interactive corporate network. Modern communication technologies will help us to improve the civil service, which must become simpler and cheaper,&quot; Chancellor Viktor Klima told a news conference.</p>
<p>Negotiations are under way between the government and civil servants, who staged two strikes last month over job cuts and pension reforms and have threatened to take further action.</p>
<p>Introducing the long-delayed internet homepage of the government, Klima said the nationwide network would connect all federal and provincial authorities.</p>
<p>&quot;Our aim is that people can get things done with our services electronically,&quot; Klima said, adding that all schools would soon be linked to the internet.</p>
<p>Austria's homepage address is &quot;http://www.austria.gv.at&quot;.</p>
<p>-- Christian Gutlederer, Vienna Newsroom, +43 1 531 12 275</p>
